      "rationale": "An r=0.967 correlation between two nominally independent measurement channels over 102 reading pairs is implausibly high for environmental physics and approaches instrumental precision. Systematic error (mis-timestamping, transcription error, accidental data duplication, or post-hoc offset misapplication during import) is more parsimonious than genuine ~100% coherence. The fact that pendulum_lab also shows a weak negative lag-5 autocorrelation (-0.156, at chance boundary) and that only ONE cross-instrument pair exceeds chance suggests channel-specific corruption rather than a global environmental effect. Most pressing: I must verify that pendulum_lab trials 301–352 were not inadvertently shifted or repeated during data entry, and that resonator_obs trials 201–240 were correctly timestamped and not derived from or cross-referenced with pendulum_lab during processing. A permutation test with proper multiple-comparison correction (accounting for all 120+ lags tested across four instruments) may reveal that r=0.967 is not statistically significant after adjusting for hypothesis shopping.",
      "evidenceFor": [
        "pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs r=0.967 at 3-day offset (n=102; chance ±0.287)"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
