      "rationale": "The singularity of the pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs pairing (no comparable coupling between other instrument pairs) argues against a global environment affecting all four channels identically. If both instruments are co-located or mounted on a common optical table, mechanical vibration transmission or thermal cross-coupling could produce correlated drifts with a 3-day time constant reflecting the thermal mass or resonance mode of the shared support. Pendulum_lab (low-frequency, temperature-sensitive) and resonator_obs (high-frequency, sensitive to mechanical stiffness changes) could each respond to ambient temperature variations with different phase lags and damping, creating a net r=0.967 if both are driven by a common slow driver. However, if the two instruments are >10 m apart or mounted on isolated platforms (as is typical in precision labs), this drops near zero. The absence of strong coupling in pendulum_obs ↔ resonator_lab (r=0.210) or pendulum_obs ↔ resonator_obs (r=0.175) suggests the effect is not a simple 'all pendulums' or 'all resonators' structure, but instrument-pair-specific, consistent with co-location. Day 40 readings show pendulum_lab trial 345 (1.7134 beats) as an outlier, while resonator_obs trial 237 (4.1434 cycles/beat) is also elevated—both slightly outside ±2σ of their respective post-baseline means—but offset by ~3 days apart in the raw event log, which could be coincidence or weak evidence of coupling.",
