        "no facility thermal or seismic monitoring data provided; co-location unconfirmed",
        "if coupling is genuine, lag should be consistent across all 38-day dataset; no periodic 3-day structure visible in raw residuals"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Overfitting and multiple-comparison bias in lag-search analysis: the r=0.967 at lag=3 is a false positive from testing ~120 lag-offset hypotheses without correction",
      "probability": 0.15,
      "rationale": "I searched all offsets from -30 to +30 days across four instruments, selecting the single best r at the best lag for each pair. This is a classic multiple-comparisons problem: if I test 120 independent null hypotheses (lag offsets × instrument pairs) at α=0.05, I expect ~6 false positives by chance, even if no true correlation exists. The r=0.967 observed a posteriori for pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs at lag=3 may well be the 1-in-120 extreme value predicted by the null. To test this, I need to (i) apply a permutation test on the original data, shuffling trials within each instrument independently and recomputing the best-lag-best-r distribution under the null, or (ii) apply a Bonferroni or FDR correction to the observed r=0.967, accounting for 120 effective tests. If the true significance level of r=0.967 with n=102 after Bonferroni correction is p>0.05, this hypothesis is supported. Degrees of freedom may also be inflated: if each trial is a composite of 10+ raw sensor samples, true n is much lower, making r=0.967 plausible under the null. Additionally, I should verify that my lag-search algorithm is not biased (e.g., does it prefer positive or negative lags?), and confirm that the offset is computed correctly (calendar days vs. trial index).",
      "evidenceFor": [
