  "residual": 0.00,
  "summaryOfChange": "I am abandoning the prior question ('why the negative lag-5 autocorr and generic day-38 anomalies?') in favor of the *single largest anomaly in the entire dataset*: the r=0.967 pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs correlation at 3-day offset. This is 2.4σ above chance and is the only cross-instrument pair to exceed the noise floor; it demands immediate investigation before any other measurement campaign proceeds. I am shifting the probability mass from the 'genuine shared environmental signal' hypothesis (p=0.38 → 0.32) because the co-location hypothesis is unconfirmed and the temporal structure in Day 40 data does not support a tight 3-day coupling. I am elevating the 'hidden systematic error' hypothesis (p=0.28 → 0.48) as the most parsimonious explanation, because r=0.967 is implausibly high for uncorrelated instruments and because no causal mechanism or facility documentation supports co-location. I have also introduced the 'overfitting' hypothesis (p=0.15) to account for multiple-comparison bias, which I omitted entirely from the prior set despite testing 120+ lag offsets—this is a methodological lapse I am correcting. The next action is: (1) verify raw pendulum_lab and resonator_obs trial logs against original instrument output files, checking for transcription errors or timestamp shifts; (2) apply permutation test with Bonferroni correction to the lag-search results; (3) obtain facility floor plans and thermal/seismic data, or document instrument co-location/separation; (4) do not publish any result derived from these instruments until one of the first three checks rules out hidden error."
